{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,220p' formal/checks.cfg\"","aggregated_output":"[options]\nisa rv32im\nnret 2\nsolver bitwuzla\n\n[depth]\n# BMC depth per check. 2nd column (where present) is the k-induction prove\n# depth. Keep depths matched to the current pipeline so bugs within ~20\n# cycles of any symbolic instruction stream are exposed.\ninsn          20\nreg       10  20\npc_fwd    10  20\npc_bwd    10  20\ncausal    10  20\n# unique   — no two retirements share rvfi_order (catches double-retire)\n# cover    — sanity that the CPU retires (prevents vacuous-pass suites)\n# ill      — illegal instructions must trap; rvfi_trap from decoder.is_illegal\n# liveness — CPU makes forward progress under any symbolic stream\nunique     1 10 20\ncover      1    20\nill             20\nliveness   1 10 20\n\n[defines]\n`define RISCV_FORMAL_ALIGNED_MEM\n# RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS — fast mode. Substitutes M-ext mul/div/rem with\n# simpler XOR-of-add/sub formulas so bitwuzla solves them inside the\n# 20-step BMC budget (rvfi.rst §7.6 \"Alternative arithmetic operations\").\n#\n# IMPORTANT — what this DOES and DOES NOT prove:\n#   ✓ proves: M-ext bypassing, operand routing (rs1/rs2 -> unit -> rd),\n#             stall/forwarding behavior on mul/div, all I-base arithmetic\n#             and trap discipline.\n#   ✗ does NOT prove: the actual MUL/DIV/REM arithmetic is correct.\n#     The DUT and the spec both implement the same XOR substitute under\n#     this define, so a wrong real multiplier can still pass formal here.\n#\n# M-extension arithmetic correctness is covered separately by:\n#   1. test/test_alu.py — 30+ vectors per M-ext op, exact bit results.\n#   2. cosim against test/cosim/reference.py — every retired MUL/DIV/REM\n#      diff'd field-by-field.\n#   3. `make formal-deep` — same checks without ALTOPS. SLOW (~hours per\n#      M-ext check) but proves real arithmetic. Run periodically, not in\n#      the per-iteration orchestrator loop.\n`define RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS\n\n[cover]\n# Sanity cover: the CPU must retire at least one non-trapping instruction.\n# A vacuous suite (nothing ever retires) would fail this — no symbolic\n# trace can make rvfi_valid && !rvfi_trap go true.\nalways @* if (!reset) cover (rvfi_valid && !rvfi_trap);\n\n[verilog-files]\n@basedir@/cores/@core@/core_pkg.sv\n@basedir@/cores/@core@/alu.sv\n@basedir@/cores/@core@/decoder.sv\n@basedir@/cores/@core@/imm_gen.sv\n@basedir@/cores/@core@/reg_file.sv\n@basedir@/cores/@core@/if_stage.sv\n@basedir@/cores/@core@/id_stage.sv\n@basedir@/cores/@core@/ex_stage.sv\n@basedir@/cores/@core@/mem_stage.sv\n@basedir@/cores/@core@/wb_stage.sv\n@basedir@/cores/@core@/hazard_unit.sv\n@basedir@/cores/@core@/forward_unit.sv\n@basedir@/cores/@core@/core.sv\n@basedir@/cores/@core@/wrapper.sv\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_12", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,260p' formal/run_all.sh\"", "aggregated_output": "#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# Run riscv-formal checks against the current $RTL_DIR/*.sv.\n# Requires: formal/riscv-formal cloned (manual clone or `git submodule init`).\n#\n# Stages every $RTL_DIR/*.sv plus wrapper.sv + the chosen checks config under\n# the riscv-formal tree, then runs sby -> bitwuzla via the framework's\n# generated makefile. Tallies PASS/FAIL by inspecting each task's\n# logfile.txt.\n#\n# Usage: bash formal/run_all.sh [checks-cfg-path]\n#   default: formal/checks.cfg       (fast, ALTOPS, used by orchestrator)\n#   deep   : formal/checks-deep.cfg  (no ALTOPS, proves M-ext arithmetic)\n#\n# Env vars (orchestrator-driven, override $1 and defaults):\n#   WRAPPER     — path to wrapper SV (default formal/wrapper.sv)\n#   CHECKS_CFG  — path to checks config (default formal/checks.cfg)\n# When BOTH are unset, the script auto-detects nret from\n# cores/$CORE_NAME/core.yaml: nret=1 routes to wrapper_si.sv +\n# checks_si.cfg; nret=2 (or absent) uses the defaults. Agents\n# invoking `bash formal/run_all.sh` directly therefore get the\n# right wrapper without needing to know the env-var plumbing.\nset -e\n\nif [ -z \"${RTL_DIR:-}\" ] || [ -z \"${CORE_NAME:-}\" ]; then\n  echo \"ERROR: formal/run_all.sh requires RTL_DIR and CORE_NAME env vars.\" >&2\n  echo \"  Example: RTL_DIR=cores/v1/rtl CORE_NAME=v1 bash formal/run_all.sh formal/checks.cfg\" >&2\n  exit 2\nfi\n\nSCRIPT_DIR=\"$(cd \"$(dirname \"$0\")\" && pwd)\"\nPROJECT_ROOT=\"$(cd \"$SCRIPT_DIR/..\" && pwd)\"\nRISCV_FORMAL=\"$SCRIPT_DIR/riscv-formal\"\n# Per-invocation work dir + log, unique among every process sharing this\n# riscv-formal checkout (the K concurrent agents of a run share it through\n# their worktrees' symlink). The name used to be \"$CORE_NAME-$$\", but an\n# agent's sandboxed shell runs in its own PID namespace where $$ is 2 or 3\n# for every agent, so siblings wrote into one dir and wiped each other's\n# checks mid-SBY (V2 incident 03). mktemp gives a unique name instead.\n# FORMAL_WORK_ID pins the suffix so tools/eval/formal.py can remove exactly\n# its own dir afterwards. genchecks.py derives @core\n…[7517 of 10589 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:a7b49395012a115d]…\nheses make the assumption satisfiable, so this\n        # branch never matches and the regular DONE (PASS branch covers it.\n        PASS=$((PASS+1))\n    else\n        FAIL=$((FAIL+1)); FAILED+=(\"$name\")\n    fi\ndone\nTOTAL=$((PASS+FAIL))\nif [ $TOTAL -eq 0 ]; then\n    FAIL=1\n    FAILED+=(\"no_checks_generated\")\nfi\n\necho \"\"\necho \"Formal: $PASS passed, $FAIL failed\"\nif [ $FAIL -gt 0 ]; then\n    echo \"Failed: ${FAILED[*]}\"\n    # Surface the FIRST failing check's logfile tail. tools/eval/formal.py\n    # captures this script's stdout/stderr into formal['detail'] and the\n    # orchestrator now records that in experiments/log.jsonl, so what we\n    # print here is what shows up as the diagnostic for the failed run.\n    first=\"${FAILED[0]}\"\n    if [ -f \"$first/logfile.txt\" ]; then\n        echo \"\"\n        echo \"--- $first/logfile.txt (last 30 lines) ---\"\n        tail -30 \"$first/logfile.txt\"\n    fi\n    echo \"\"\n    echo \"Full run log: $LOG\"\n    echo \"Per-check logs in: $CORE_DIR/checks/<check>/logfile.txt\"\nfi\n[ $FAIL -eq 0 ]\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
