{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_60","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,220p' cores/bench/implementation_notes.md\"","aggregated_output":"## What changed\n\n- Removed the signed, unsigned, and mixed-signed high-half products from the\n  combinational ALU fast path. `ALU_MUL` remains single-cycle low-half multiply.\n- Added `mulhi_unit.sv`, a registered slow unit for `MULH`, `MULHU`, and\n  `MULHSU`. It captures operands, computes the exact RV32M high word, and\n  pulses `done` for `ex_stage`.\n- Updated `ex_stage.sv` so high-half multiply variants reuse the existing\n  `ex_busy` stall/bubble flow and feed their completed result through the same\n  EX/MEM `alu_result` path as divider results.\n- Added focused cocotb coverage for `mulhi_unit` edge cases and a pipeline\n  forwarding test for a slow `MULH` followed by a dependent `ADD`.\n\n## Deviations\n\n- The high-half multiply unit uses a two-step registered flow rather than a\n  single combinational EX result. This more fully removes forwarded operand\n  signedness/product logic from the hot ALU-to-EX/MEM path.\n\n## Concerns\n\n- High-half multiplies now take extra cycles by design. This should be fine for\n  CoreMark's common low-half `MUL` path, but code with dense `MULH*` sequences\n  will lose IPC.\n- Manual `make cosim TARGET=bench` still defaults the harness to `NRET=2`, while\n  `cores/bench/core.yaml` declares `nret: 1`. The matching manual invocation is\n  `make cosim TARGET=bench NRET=1`.\n\n## Local checks\n\n- `verilator --lint-only -Wall -Wno-MULTITOP -sv +incdir+cores/bench/rtl cores/bench/rtl/*.sv`: pass.\n- `RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl CORE_NAME=bench bash formal/run_all.sh`: pass, 53 passed / 0 failed.\n- `pytest -q cores/bench/test/test_alu.py cores/bench/test/test_mulhi_unit.py cores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py`: pass, 3 passed.\n- `make cosim TARGET=bench NRET=1`: pass, 2 ELFs tested.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
