{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_36","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,220p' cores/bench/implementation_notes.md\"","aggregated_output":"Specialize low-half MUL product\n================================\n\nWhat changed\n------------\n- `rtl/alu.sv` now computes `ALU_MUL` from a dedicated truncated low-half\n  product:\n  - low-low 16x16 partial product\n  - low 16 bits of the low-high cross partial\n  - low 16 bits of the high-low cross partial\n  - no high-high partial term, because it begins at product bit 32\n- `ALU_MULH`, `ALU_MULHU`, and `ALU_MULHSU` continue to use the existing\n  signed/unsigned full 64-bit product rails.\n- `RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS` behavior is unchanged.\n- `test/test_alu.py` now checks `ALU_MUL` with zero, all-ones, high-bit,\n  cross-term, and mixed-pattern operands.\n\nDeviations\n----------\n- None from the hypothesis intent. The change stays local to the ALU and\n  does not add stalls, forwarding paths, or control metadata.\n\nConcerns\n--------\n- The low-half path adds extra 16x16 multiply logic while leaving the full\n  unsigned product in place for `MULHU`, so this intentionally trades some\n  area for a shorter `ALU_MUL` result cone.\n- Plain `make cosim TARGET=bench` defaulted the C++ harness to `NRET=2` and\n  failed to build against this `nret: 1` core before simulation. Rerunning\n  with `NRET=1` matched `core.yaml` and passed.\n\nLocal checks\n------------\n- Verilator lint: pass\n  `verilator --lint-only -Wall -Wno-MULTITOP -sv +incdir+cores/bench/rtl cores/bench/rtl/*.sv`\n- ALU cocotb test: pass\n  `pytest -q cores/bench/test/test_alu.py`\n- Formal: pass, 53 passed / 0 failed\n  `RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl CORE_NAME=bench bash formal/run_all.sh`\n- Cosim: pass, 2 ELFs tested\n  `NRET=1 make cosim TARGET=bench`\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
