{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_13", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'tail -n 5 cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'", "aggregated_output": "{\"id\": \"hyp-20261004-001-r3s0\", \"title\": \"Share the single-cycle multiplier cone\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current champion keeps MUL on the single-cycle path, which prior runs show is important for CoreMark IPC, but alu.sv still builds three independent 64-bit multiplier products for MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU on every ALU evaluation.  The current worst path remains inside the ID/EX to EX/MEM execute cone, so reducing duplicated multiply hardware and its result muxing is a plausible Fmax win without changing the pipeline schedule.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current champion keeps MUL on the single-cycle path, which prior runs show is important for CoreMark IPC, but alu.sv still builds three independent 64-bit multiplier products for MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU on every ALU evaluation.  The current worst path remains inside the ID/EX to EX/MEM execute cone, so reducing duplicated multiply hardware and its result muxing is a plausible Fmax win without changing the pipeline schedule.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the three parallel multiplier expressions in the combinational ALU with one shared single-cycle product.  Drive that product with 33-bit sign- or zero-extended operands selected from the MUL variant, then use the low 32 bits for MUL and bits [63:32] for MULH/MULHU/MULHSU.  This preserves exact RV32M results and CoreMark's single-cycle multiply throughput while removing duplicated DSP/LUT/routing load from the execute cone; DIV/REM stay on the existing iterative divider.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Collapse mul_ss, mul_uu, and mul_su into one op-selected 33-bit signed product and select the required low or high result bits for the