{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_17", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '10,12p' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"", "aggregated_output": "{\"id\": \"hyp-20261004-003-r3s2\", \"title\": \"Tiny learned loop-target predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The prior predictor attempts showed that CoreMark can benefit from fewer\\ntaken-control bubbles, but the implementations lost too much Fmax by putting\\nBTB tables, IF-stage immediate decode, target adders, and predicted-next-PC\\nvalidation into already-sensitive control paths. The most promising remaining\\npredictor angle is to capture the same hot loop back-edges with far less\\nfetch logic.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The prior predictor attempts showed that CoreMark can benefit from fewer\\ntaken-control bubbles, but the implementations lost too much Fmax by putting\\nBTB tables, IF-stage immediate decode, target adders, and predicted-next-PC\\nvalidation into already-sensitive control paths. The most promising remaining\\npredictor angle is to capture the same hot loop back-edges with far less\\nfetch logic.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a tiny exact-tag dynamic loop-target predictor that learns only real\\ntaken backward conditional branches in EX. On each taken branch with a\\nnegative immediate, update one or two registered entries containing the\\nbranch PC and resolved target. In IF, prediction is only a PC-tag compare\\nagainst those entries and a mux from pc+4 to the stored target; there is no\\ninstruction decode, B-immediate reconstruction, counter table, or target\\nadder in fetch. Carry only a predicted-taken bit into ID/EX. EX keeps the\\nexisting architectural branch resolution and redirects on the two mismatch\\ncases: predicted taken but actually not taken redirects to pc+4, while not\\npredicted but actually taken redirects to the resolved target. This should\\nrecover most repeated loop-backedge bubbles seen by the failed static\\npredictor while avoiding the long IF/EX plumbing that caused its Fmax loss.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a single predicted_taken metadata bit to IF/ID and ID/EX p\n…[11797 of 14869 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:cd58e6f694607216]…\nax_mhz\": 55.487, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4491817, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 2732, \"ff\": 694, \"seeds\": [54.534, 55.487, 55.487], \"perturbations\": [[0, 54.534, 13], [1, 55.487, 13], [2, 55.487, 13]], \"lutram\": 8, \"bsram\": 6, \"dsp\": 6, \"critical_path\": {\"from\": \"cpu/u_rf/regs_regs_0_0_s0\", \"to\": \"cpu/u_ex/reg_q.alu_result_16_s0\", \"slack_ns\": -13.337, \"arrival_ns\": 23.632, \"logic_cells\": 13, \"modules\": [\"clock_ibuf\", \"cpu/u_id\", \"cpu/u_ex\", \"cpu/u_ex/u_alu\", \"cpu/u_ex\"]}, \"logic_levels\": 13, \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-10-04T07:00:24.636693Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 4, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-10-04 hyp-20261004-002-r4s1 (improvement, +7.50%): Splitting LOAD/STORE rs1+imm onto a latched EX/MEM effective_addr rail improves timing by letting dmem address, mask, alignment, load extraction, and RVFI mem_addr bypass the full ALU/result mux cone, but loads must be blocked from EX/MEM forwarding because their data is not available there.\"}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
