{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_3", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,220p' cores/bench/LESSONS.md\"", "aggregated_output": "- 2026-10-04 hyp-20261004-001-r1s0 (improvement, +728.80%): Moving DIV/REM off the single-cycle ALU path can massively improve CoreMark timing when divides are rare, because SystemVerilog \"/\" and \"%\" otherwise put a full 32-bit divider/remainder behind the forwarding muxes.\n- 2026-10-04 hyp-20261004-002-r1s1 (regression, +415.68%): Splitting all RV32M ops into a front-end-stalling multi-cycle unit regresses CoreMark because its MUL-heavy hot loops lose single-cycle throughput; keep MUL on the fast path and move only rare DIV/REM off the EX ALU cone.\n- 2026-10-04 hyp-20261004-003-r1s2 (regression, -1.24%): A 16-entry direct-mapped BTB/counter next-PC predictor did not repay its IF-stage lookup and predicted-PC plumbing on CoreMark, so front-end predictor cost must be measured before assuming hot loops will offset EX redirect penalties.\n- 2026-10-04 hyp-20261004-001-r2s0 (improvement, +14.40%): Removing EX redirect from the IF/ID instr/valid mux improved timing because ID's redirect flush already kills the wrong-path instruction; mask load-use while redirect is asserted so stale rs1/rs2 cannot create a spurious stall.\n- 2026-10-04 hyp-20261004-002-r2s1 (regression, -4.16%): Registering IF/ID deepened the front end and made redirects/front-end bubbles carry an extra valid/kill stage, so CoreMark's branch/control cost outweighed any timing relief from cutting the IF-to-hazard loop.\n- 2026-10-04 hyp-20261004-003-r2s2 (regression, -12.09%): Table-free backward-taken prediction still regressed CoreMark because IF-stage B-immediate decode/pc+imm selection plus pred_next_pc compare plumbing lengthened the fetch/control path more than saved back-edge redirect bubbles.\n- 2026-10-04 hyp-20261004-001-r3s0 (regression, +0.44%): Sharing MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU through one op-selected 33x33 signed product can add sign-extension muxing on the execute cone that outweighs removing apparently duplicated multiplier expressions.\n- 2026-10-04 hyp-20261004-002-r3s1 (regression, -12.60%): A tiny sequential IF FIFO did not hide CoreMark's r\n…[4816 of 7888 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:82655ac0e1379c19]…\n accepted target by branch resolution and correct hits become redirect/flush repairs; repair needs an age-aligned next-PC token or stored target.\n- 2026-10-04 hyp-20261004-001-r9s0 (regression, -0.52%): Selectively clearing only pipeline control bits on reset/flush can still fan reset/flush/div-bubble conditions into wide data flops as hold enables or feedback muxes, so invalid-bubble don't-care rails need true ungated updates to buy timing.\n- 2026-10-04 hyp-20261004-002-r9s1 (placement_failed, n/a): Posted-store arbitration that muxes `io_dmemAddr/WData/WEn` between EX/MEM and a buffered store can break Gowin's `core_bench_si` dmem RAM inference, turning byte lane `dmem_3` into 16,384 DFFs and exceeding the Tang Nano 20K FF limit.\n- 2026-10-04 hyp-20261004-003-r9s2 (regression, -2.16%): Even with only a `predicted_taken` bit carried to EX, a one-entry loop predictor still pays IF-side branch decode, B-immediate fingerprint compare, and target mux cost that CoreMark back-edge bubble savings did not amortize.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
