{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_21","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261004-001-r11s0.yaml')\nprint(p.read_text())\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261004-001-r11s0\ntitle: \"Interlock EX-forwarded MUL operands\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The accepted core is now limited by a short but stubborn execute path through\n  forwarding, the EX operand muxes, and the single-cycle multiply rail. Prior\n  attempts show that making all MULs slower, moving MUL to MEM, or precomputing\n  forwarding selects costs too much CoreMark throughput or decode timing. The\n  remaining narrow opportunity is the immediate EX/MEM-to-MUL bypass case: it\n  keeps the producer rd compare, forwarded result mux, operand mux, and DSP input\n  in one timed cone even though many CoreMark multiplies already get operands\n  from ID/EX or MEM/WB after load-use and memory-stall spacing.\nhypothesis: |\n  Treat a multiply whose rs1 or rs2 would be supplied by the immediately\n  preceding EX/MEM producer as a one-cycle interlock, then feed the multiplier\n  only from the latched ID/EX operands or the existing MEM/WB forwarding path.\n  Normal EX/MEM forwarding remains available for simple ALU ops, branch\n  compares, load/store address generation, store data, and all non-multiply\n  consumers; low-half MUL and high-half MUL variants stay single-cycle whenever\n  their operands are already available.\n\n  Removing the EX/MEM forwarded-result arm from the dedicated multiply operand\n  mux should cut the static path that recent timing reports name as\n  cpu/u_fwd -> cpu/u_ex -> cpu/u_ex/u_alu -> reg_q.mul_result. CoreMark should\n  pay bubbles only for adjacent ALU/JAL-to-MUL dependencies, not for every MUL,\n  so a modest Fmax gain can clear the replacement threshold without repeating\n  the broad multiplier-pipelining regressions.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: \"Add a MUL-specific adjacent-producer hazard that injects a single bubble when the next instruction would need EX/MEM forwarding into a multiply operand, with the same redirect, dmem-stall, and ex_busy priorities as load-use.\"\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \"Split multiply operand selection from the normal ALU/branch operand muxes so the multiplier can use ID/EX or MEM/WB operands but not the EX/MEM forwarded-result rail.\"\n  - file: rtl/alu.sv\n    description: \"Expose or factor the multiply datapath so the core can drive it with the dedicated no-EX/MEM-bypass operands while preserving the legacy op-selected ALU output for standalone tests.\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Wire the MUL interlock inputs from IF/ID source fields and the current ID/EX producer metadata, while keeping existing forwarding for non-multiply consumers unchanged.\"\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: \"Add small helper metadata or local encodings only if needed to identify multiply consumers and producer-forwardability cleanly across the hazard and execute logic.\"\n  - file: test/test_mul_forward_interlock.py\n    description: \"Cover adjacent ALU/JAL-to-MUL interlocks, load-to-MUL behavior, MEM/WB-to-MUL forwarding, back-to-back independent MULs, high-half variants, redirects, and dmem stalls around the new bubble.\"\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
