{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_6", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor line in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n    j=json.loads(line)\n    if j.get('category')=='micro_opt':\n        print('\\\\n###', j['id'], j['title'])\n        print('outcome', j.get('outcome'), 'fitness', j.get('fitness'), 'fmax', j.get('fmax_mhz'), 'delta', j.get('delta_pct'))\n        print('hyp:', str(j.get('hypothesis',{}).get('hypothesis', j.get('hypothesis','')))[:800].replace('\\\\n',' '))\n        print('notes:', str(j.get('implementation_notes',''))[:900].replace('\\\\n',' '))\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "\n### baseline-bench-6c6ea97 Baseline retest for cores/bench\noutcome improvement fitness 12.12 fmax 5.442 delta 0.0\nhyp: \nnotes: \n\n### hyp-20261004-001-r1s0 Move DIV/REM off the single-cycle ALU path\noutcome improvement fitness 100.45 fmax 45.121 delta 728.8\nhyp: Keep the RV32I ALU and MUL/MULH variants single-cycle, but replace the combinational DIV/REM cases with a small iterative divide unit in execute. When a DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU enters EX, freeze upstream pipeline state until the quotient/remainder is ready, then let the instruction advance normally with the exact RV32M result and RVFI metadata. This removes the long divider and modulo logic from the cycle-time path scored by static timing, while the IPC cost should be negligible for CoreMark if timed-region divides are rare. \nnotes: \n\n### hyp-20261004-001-r2s0 Remove redirect from fetch-decode mux\noutcome improvement fitness 114.91 fmax 51.614 delta 14.4\nhyp: Keep the exact architectural branch/jump recovery behavior, but remove redirect from the combinational IF/ID instruction and valid mux. Let redirect update the PC as it does today and rely on the existing ID-stage flush to kill the wrong-path instruction before it enters ID/EX. In the hazard unit, mask the load-use detector while redirect is asserted so a wrong-path instruction cannot create the spurious stall that the IF-stage NOP mux was originally avoiding. This should shorten the post-DIV critical path with negligible area change and no CoreMark IPC loss. \nnotes: \n\n### hyp-20261004-001-r3s0 Share the single-cycle multiplier cone\noutcome regression fitness 115.41 fmax 51.839 delta 0.44\nhyp: Replace the three parallel multiplier expressions in the combinational ALU with one shared single-cycle product.  Drive that product with 33-bit sign- or zero-extended operands selected from the MUL variant, then use the low 32 bits for MUL and bits [63:32] for MULH/MULHU/MULHSU.  This preserves exact RV32M results and CoreMark's single-cycle multiply throughput while removing duplicated DSP/LUT/routing load from the\n…[8827 of 11899 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:8941c62b5084a147]…\n32-bit NOP select no longer sit in front of decoder/regfile source extraction and the load-use compare loop. It is a local control-path cleanup with no CoreMark IPC loss, no predictor metadata, and no change to the RVFI/top-level contract. \nnotes: \n\n### hyp-20261004-001-r14s0 Retimed rare M-extension slow path\noutcome regression fitness 146.01 fmax 65.585 delta 3.15\nhyp: Keep ALU_MUL as the only single-cycle multiply result and move MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU onto the existing execute slow-operation path. At the same time, retime divider.sv so the final restoring divide iteration only latches the raw quotient/remainder and a compact finish state; perform sign correction, remainder/quotient selection, and result_q update from registered raw values in the following cycle. This combines the small Fmax gain observed from removing high-half products from the hot mul_result rail with a targeted cut of the divider path that became the limiter afterward, without slowing the common CoreMark low-half MUL stream. \nnotes: \n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
