{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,120p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261004-001-r4s0.yaml\"","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261004-001-r4s0\ntitle: \"Elide store-data load-use bubbles\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The current champion has already removed the divider from the hot ALU path, and the recent predictor and prefetch attempts show that extra front-end control is expensive on this FPGA.  The remaining low-risk opportunity is IPC: the current load-use interlock stalls on any match against the following instruction's rs1 or rs2 fields, even when the consumer is a STORE whose rs2 data is not needed until MEM.  It can also stall on rs2 bit-fields for instructions that do not actually read rs2.\nhypothesis: |\n  Refine the load-use hazard so it only bubbles when the next instruction needs the loaded value in EX.  A load followed by a store that uses the loaded register only as store data should advance without the one-cycle bubble, then forward the MEM/WB result into the store write-data path in MEM.  Use the same forwarding-valid convention as the current EX bypass, where MEM/WB data fields remain usable across dmem stalls even after retirement valid is cleared.  Add simple opcode-based source-use masks so OP-IMM, LOAD, JALR, LUI, AUIPC, JAL, FENCE, and EBREAK do not create false rs2 load-use hazards.  This preserves the existing five-stage schedule and single-cycle MUL path while recovering cycles in CoreMark's load/store-heavy code with only a small mux/comparator on the store-data side.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: \"Make load-use detection source-aware: stall for rs1 consumers and for rs2 consumers that need the value in EX, but allow load-to-store-data pairs to proceed.\"\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: \"Add a MEM-stage store-data forwarding mux from the retained MEM/WB writeback value when a store's rs2 matches the just-loaded destination, including held dmem-stall cycles.\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Wire the MEM/WB forwarding enable, destination, and existing writeback mux result into mem_stage without changing the nret=1 top-level contract.\"\n  - file: test/test_load_store_forward.py\n    description: \"Cover load-to-store-data forwarding, load-to-store-address stalls, OP-IMM false-rs2 hazards, and behavior under dmem stalls.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
