{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_8", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"jq -r 'select(.category==\\\"structural\\\") | [.id,.title,.outcome,(.delta_pct//\\\"n/a\\\"),(.fitness//\\\"n/a\\\"),(.fmax_mhz//\\\"n/a\\\"),(.cycles//\\\"n/a\\\"),(.critical_path.modules|join(\\\" > \\\")? // \\\"\\\"),(.lesson//\\\"\\\")] | @tsv' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261004-002-r1s1\tSplit M-extension out of the EX ALU\tregression\t415.68\t62.5\t47.45\t7592564\tclock_ibuf > cpu/u_id > cpu/u_ex > cpu/u_if > cpu/u_hazard > cpu/u_id\t- 2026-10-04 hyp-20261004-002-r1s1 (regression, +415.68%): Splitting all RV32M ops into a front-end-stalling multi-cycle unit regresses CoreMark because its MUL-heavy hot loops lose single-cycle throughput; keep MUL on the fast path and move only rare DIV/REM off the EX ALU cone.\nhyp-20261004-002-r2s1\tRegister the IF/ID boundary\tregression\t-4.16\t96.27\t46.258\t4805217\tclock_ibuf > cpu/u_id > cpu/u_ex > cpu/u_ex/u_alu > cpu/u_ex\t- 2026-10-04 hyp-20261004-002-r2s1 (regression, -4.16%): Registering IF/ID deepened the front end and made redirects/front-end bubbles carry an extra valid/kill stage, so CoreMark's branch/control cost outweighed any timing relief from cutting the IF-to-hazard loop.\nhyp-20261004-002-r3s1\tAdd a tiny instruction prefetch FIFO\tregression\t-12.6\t100.43\t43.574\t4338935\tclock_ibuf > cpu/u_id > cpu/u_ex > cpu/u_if > cpu/u_hazard > cpu/u_id > cpu/u_if\t- 2026-10-04 hyp-20261004-002-r3s1 (regression, -12.60%): A tiny sequential IF FIFO did not hide CoreMark's random imem stalls because fetch has little surplus bandwidth beyond back-end holds and redirects flush queued fall-throughs, while FIFO valid/head/consume control still lengthens the front-end path.\nhyp-20261004-002-r4s1\tSplit execute AGU from ALU result rail\timprovement\t7.5\t123.53\t55.487\t4491817\tclock_ibuf > cpu/u_id > cpu/u_ex > cpu/u_ex/u_alu > cpu/u_ex\t- 2026-10-04 hyp-20261004-002-r4s1 (improvement, +7.50%): Splitting LOAD/STORE rs1+imm onto a latched EX/MEM effective_addr rail improves timing by letting dmem address, mask, alignment, load extraction, and RVFI mem_addr bypass the full ALU/result mux cone, but loads must be blocked from EX/MEM forwarding because their data is not available there.\nhyp-20261004-002-r5s1\tLate-select split execute result rails\timprovement\t14.59\t141.55\t63.581\t4491817\tclock_ibuf > cpu/u_id > cpu/u_ex > cpu/u_ex/u_alu > cpu/u_ex\t- 2026-10-04 hyp-20261004-002-r5s1 (improvement, +14.59%): Carrying registered simple/MUL/DIV/JAL result rails plus a compact `wb_sel` through EX/MEM and MEM/WB improves timing because forwarding and writeback select among boundary-registered values instead of driving the EX/MEM setup path through the full ALU/MUL/DIV/JAL mux cone.\nhyp-20261004-002-r6s1\tPipeline the execute multiplier\tregression\t-6.65\t132.13\t60.586\t4585414\tclock_ibuf > cpu/u_fwd > cpu/u_ex > cpu/u_if > cpu/u_id\t- 2026-10-04 hyp-20261004-002-r6s1 (regression, -6.65%): Even a one-bubble registered `mul_pipe` sidecar regresses because CoreMark executes enough MULs that `ex_busy` stalls outweigh pulling the 32x32 cone off the forwarded EX-to-EX/MEM `mul_result` path.\nhyp-20261004-002-r7s1\tLate-select multiply variant rails\tregression\t1.41\t143.55\t64.478\t4491817\tclock_ibuf > cpu/u_id > cpu/u_ex > cpu/u_if > cpu/u_id\t- 2026-10-04 hyp-20261004-002-r7s1 (regression, +1.41%): Splitting MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU into separate EX/MEM rails can regress because the shared DSP products still exist while extra high-half flops and a forwarding/writeback variant mux add routing to a multiply path whose pre-register op select was not the binding delay.\nhyp-20261004-002-r8s1\tMove multiply onto the MEM-stage rail\tregression\t-6.08\t132.95\t60.195\t4527646\tclock_ibuf > cpu/u_id > cpu/u_ex > cpu/u_if > cpu/u_id\t- 2026-10-04 hyp-20261004-002-r8s1 (regression, -6.08%): Moving MUL/MULH onto the MEM-stage rail preserves one-per-cycle issue but still regresses CoreMark because removing `WB_SEL_MUL` EX/MEM forwarding turns immediate multiply consumers into load-like `id_ex_late_result` bubbles.\nhyp-20261004-002-r9s1\tPosted store buffer for dmem stalls\tplacement_failed\tn/a\tn/a\tn/a\tn/a\t\t- 2026-10-04 hyp-20261004-002-r9s1 (placement_failed, n/a): Posted-store arbitration that muxes `io_dmemAddr/WData/WEn` between EX/MEM and a buffered store can break Gowin's `core_bench_si` dmem RAM inference, turning byte lane `dmem_3` into 16,384 DFFs and exceeding the Tang Nano 20K FF limit.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"jq -r 'select(.id==\\\"hyp-20261004-002-r5s1\\\" or .id==\\\"hyp-20261004-002-r4s1\\\" or .id==\\\"hyp-20261004-002-r2s1\\\" or .id==\\\"hyp-20261004-002-r9s1\\\") | .id+\\\"\\\\nTITLE: \\\"+.title+\\\"\\\\nCATEGORY: \\\"+.category+\\\"\\\\nHYPOTHESIS:\\\\n\\\"+(.hypothesis.hypothesis // .hypothesis)+\\\"\\\\nNOTES:\\\\n\\\"+(.implementation_notes // \\\"\\\")+\\\"\\\\nCRIT: \\\"+((.critical_path.modules|join(\\\" > \\\"))? // \\\"\\\")+\\\"\\\\n\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
