{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_9", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"jq -r 'select(.id==\\\"hyp-20261004-002-r5s1\\\" or .id==\\\"hyp-20261004-002-r4s1\\\" or .id==\\\"hyp-20261004-002-r2s1\\\" or .id==\\\"hyp-20261004-002-r9s1\\\") | .id+\\\"\\\\nTITLE: \\\"+.title+\\\"\\\\nCATEGORY: \\\"+.category+\\\"\\\\nHYPOTHESIS:\\\\n\\\"+(.hypothesis.hypothesis // .hypothesis)+\\\"\\\\nNOTES:\\\\n\\\"+(.implementation_notes // \\\"\\\")+\\\"\\\\nCRIT: \\\"+((.critical_path.modules|join(\\\" > \\\"))? // \\\"\\\")+\\\"\\\\n\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261004-002-r2s1\nTITLE: Register the IF/ID boundary\nCATEGORY: structural\nHYPOTHESIS:\nTurn IF/ID into a real registered pipeline boundary with explicit valid,\nenable, and kill behavior. Fetch should capture pc/instruction only when the\ninstruction bus is ready and the downstream stage can accept it, hold the\nregistered payload during dmem/divider/load-use stalls, and clear only the\nvalid bit on redirect or front-end bubble. The hazard unit should compare\nload-use hazards against the registered IF/ID instruction and its valid bit\ninstead of a redirect-masked combinational instruction coming out of IF.\nRedirect can still update the PC immediately and flush the two younger\ninstructions, but the EX-to-IF-to-hazard-to-ID same-cycle feedback path is\nremoved. The expected win is higher Fmax from a cleaner register-to-register\ncontrol boundary while preserving single-cycle MUL throughput and the current\nsingle-retire RVFI contract.\n\nNOTES:\n\nCRIT: clock_ibuf > cpu/u_id > cpu/u_ex > cpu/u_ex/u_alu > cpu/u_ex\n\nhyp-20261004-002-r4s1\nTITLE: Split execute AGU from ALU result rail\nCATEGORY: structural\nHYPOTHESIS:\nAdd a dedicated execute-stage address-generation rail for LOAD and STORE\ninstructions. Compute effective_addr = forwarded_rs1 + imm beside the\nnormal ALU, latch it in EX/MEM, and have MEM use that field for dmem_addr,\nbyte-lane masks, misalignment checks, load extraction, and RVFI mem_addr.\nKeep the existing ALU rail for arithmetic writeback and keep MUL single-cycle;\nDIV/REM remain on the current iterative path. This should let the timed\ndmem-address path bypass the full ALU result case tree and multiplier result\nmuxing without adding CoreMark cycles, while preserving exact memory semantics\nand the nret=1 RVFI contract.\n\nNOTES:\n\nCRIT: clock_ibuf > cpu/u_id > cpu/u_ex > cpu/u_ex/u_alu > cpu/u_ex\n\nhyp-20261004-002-r5s1\nTITLE: Late-select split execute result rails\nCATEGORY: structural\nHYPOTHESIS:\nSplit the execute writeback result path into registered op-class rails and move the final result selection after the EX/MEM boundary. Keep a compact simple-ALU rail for RV32I arithmetic and logic, keep single-cycle multiply on its own rail, keep jump return-address and divider-complete values on separate rails, and carry a small writeback-select code through MEM/WB so wb_stage and EX/MEM forwarding choose among already-registered results. This removes the large ALU case tree / MUL / DIV / JAL mux from the setup path into reg_q.alu_result while preserving single-cycle MUL throughput, current load/store AGU behavior, RVFI order, and CoreMark IPC.\n\nNOTES:\n\nCRIT: clock_ibuf > cpu/u_id > cpu/u_ex > cpu/u_ex/u_alu > cpu/u_ex\n\nhyp-20261004-002-r9s1\nTITLE: Posted store buffer for dmem stalls\nCATEGORY: structural\nHYPOTHESIS:\nAdd a one-entry posted store buffer at the MEM boundary.  When a legal store\nreaches MEM while dmem is not ready, capture its address, replicated data,\nmask, and RVFI metadata, let the store retire once into MEM/WB, and allow\nyounger non-memory instructions to continue while the buffer drains to the\ndmem port on the first ready cycle.  Preserve simple in-order semantics by\nstalling any younger load, any younger store while the single buffer entry is\noccupied, and any ordered side-effect instruction until the buffered write is\nvisible.  Ordered side effects include UART/marker MMIO stores, FENCE/FENCE.I,\nEBREAK, and trapping instructions.  This should hide a meaningful fraction of\nCoreMark's random store stalls without touching the single-cycle multiply path\nor adding predictor feedback into fetch.\n\nNOTES:\n\nCRIT: \n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,220p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261004-002-r10s1.yaml\"","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261004-002-r10s1\ntitle: \"Register branch redirect control\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The current champion is no longer dominated by the old divide cone; recent\n  timing paths repeatedly run through forwarding into execute and then back\n  through fetch/decode control before landing in ID/EX. Predictor attempts\n  made this loop worse, while deeper IF/ID registration cost too many branch\n  cycles. A narrower structural cut at the execute-to-fetch redirect boundary\n  may improve Fmax without changing steady-state issue width or MUL latency.\nhypothesis: |\n  Split branch and jump recovery into a registered one-cycle redirect stage:\n  execute computes the resolved next PC and whether a redirect is required,\n  but the IF PC mux and ID flush consume a registered redirect token on the\n  following cycle. In the same cycle that EX resolves a taken branch or jump,\n  allow the current wrong-path instruction to enter ID/EX as a poisoned bubble\n  by clearing only its side-effect controls and valid bit. This intentionally\n  adds one extra bubble to taken redirects, but should remove the long\n  same-cycle EX -> IF -> hazard -> ID feedback path that has shown up in\n  failed predictor attempts and several worst-path reports. CoreMark's branch\n  penalty may rise modestly, yet a clean Fmax gain above the 4.6 percent\n  acceptance threshold is plausible if the redirect control loop is the\n  current limiter.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: \"Add a compact redirect token/poison metadata bit to the pipeline bundles if needed for age-aligned one-cycle redirect recovery.\"\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \"Register resolved redirect_valid and redirect_target rather than driving fetch and hazard recovery directly from same-cycle EX combinational logic.\"\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: \"Consume the registered redirect token for PC update, keeping the normal sequential fetch path otherwise unchanged.\"\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: \"Convert redirect recovery into a side-effect-free poisoned ID/EX bubble when the registered redirect token arrives.\"\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: \"Retarget flush and load-use masking to the registered redirect token while preserving dmem stall and divider-busy priority.\"\n  - file: test/test_registered_redirect.py\n    description: \"Cover taken branches, jumps, misaligned traps, and redirects coincident with imem/dmem stalls so the delayed redirect cannot retire wrong-path work.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"agent_message","text":"Structural history says the accepted wins were very local: split the address/result rails, keep MUL single-cycle, avoid wider front-end machinery. The remaining worthwhile question is whether to chase Fmax with a control cut or IPC with a carefully scoped bypass; I’m checking the memory wrapper before deciding."}}
