{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,220p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261004-002-r11s1.yaml\"","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261004-002-r11s1\ntitle: \"Tiny direct-mapped I-cache\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The current champion keeps single-cycle MUL throughput and has already removed\n  the largest execute result muxes, but CoreMark still pays the externally\n  generated instruction-bus stall stream on every fetch. A prior IF FIFO could\n  not help because the frontend rarely has surplus bandwidth and redirects\n  discard queued fall-throughs. A cache is different: CoreMark repeatedly\n  executes the same hot loop PCs, so after the first miss those instructions can\n  be served locally even when io_imemReady is low.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add a small direct-mapped instruction cache inside if_stage, for example 64 or\n  128 single-word lines with valid bits and PC tags. The fetch stage should treat\n  a cache hit as an instruction-ready event independent of io_imemReady, using\n  the cached word for IF/ID while still updating/filling the line whenever the\n  external imem returns ready for the current PC. On a miss, preserve today's\n  behavior: hold the PC, emit a bubble, and fill the selected line when\n  io_imemReady arrives.\n\n  This keeps the five-stage schedule, RVFI retirement order, static not-taken\n  branch policy, and single-cycle multiply path unchanged. It targets IPC rather\n  than another fragile execute retiming: hot CoreMark loops should stop losing\n  roughly one frontend bubble per random imem stall after warmup, while the added\n  IF tag compare and instruction mux are smaller than the failed predictor\n  designs because there is no target table, predicted-PC metadata, or EX repair\n  validation path.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 8\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: \"Add a tiny direct-mapped instruction cache, return cached instructions on hits, and generate an ifetch-ready indication that hides io_imemReady on cache hits.\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Wire the fetch-stage ready indication into the existing hazard logic while leaving the top-level imem and RVFI port contracts unchanged.\"\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: \"Use the effective ifetch-ready signal rather than raw io_imemReady for frontend stall and bubble decisions.\"\n  - file: test/test_icache.py\n    description: \"Cover cache hits under imem stalls, miss fill behavior, redirects to cached and uncached PCs, and reset invalidation.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
