{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,220p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261004-002-r13s1.yaml\"","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261004-002-r13s1\ntitle: \"EX-registered load-cache probe\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The best structural change so far is the split execute result rail, and the closest miss is the tiny LSU load cache: it improved cycles and Fmax enough to reach +4.25%, just below the acceptance threshold.  That result suggests CoreMark does have useful data locality under the random dmem stall stream, but the MEM-stage hit compare, cached-vs-bus load mux, and effective-ready plumbing were still too late and too broad.\nhypothesis: |\n  Keep the same architectural idea of satisfying repeated RAM loads locally, but restructure the LSU so the cache lookup is launched from the registered EX address path and its hit/data result is carried as a registered EX/MEM sideband into MEM.  MEM then sees only a cheap registered hit bit and registered cached word: on a hit it suppresses the external read and treats the load as ready even when io_dmemReady is low; on a miss it keeps the current blocking path and fills or updates the cache only after the external access completes.\n\n  This should preserve most of the IPC gain from the previous load-hit cache while removing the same-cycle tag/data lookup from the load extraction and hazard-ready path.  Stores, MMIO, marker addresses, and misaligned accesses remain blocking and uncached, with same-line stores invalidating or updating the cached word after the external write completes.  The goal is to cross the champion threshold by combining the prior cache's stall hiding with cleaner timing placement rather than adding another front-end predictor or FIFO.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: \"Add registered LSU cache-probe sideband fields to the EX/MEM payload for cacheable, hit, cached word, and tag/index metadata.\"\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \"Launch the load-cache probe from the effective address rail and latch the hit/data result beside the existing EX/MEM memory metadata without changing ALU or MUL forwarding latency.\"\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: \"Consume the registered probe result to select cached load data, suppress external reads on hits, fill or invalidate/update entries after completed external RAM accesses, and keep MMIO/misaligned/store behavior blocking.\"\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: \"Base dmem backpressure on the registered LSU hit/miss outcome so cache-hit loads do not freeze the pipeline when raw io_dmemReady is low.\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Wire the LSU cache probe and effective-ready signals between EX, MEM, and the hazard unit while preserving the nret=1 top-level IO contract.\"\n  - file: test/test_lsu_cache_probe.py\n    description: \"Cover hit-under-stall loads, miss fills, store invalidation or update, uncached MMIO and marker accesses, byte and halfword extraction, reset invalidation, and redirects around cached loads.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
