sign-extension muxing on the execute cone that outweighs removing apparently duplicated multiplier expressions.\n- 2026-10-04 hyp-20261004-002-r3s1 (regression, -12.60%): A tiny sequential IF FIFO did not hide CoreMark's random imem stalls because fetch has little surplus bandwidth beyond back-end holds and redirects flush queued fall-throughs, while FIFO valid/head/consume control still lengthens the front-end path.\n- 2026-10-04 hyp-20261004-003-r3s2 (regression, +0.40%): Even an exact two-entry learned loop-target predictor without IF decode or target adders barely regressed, suggesting CoreMark back-edge bubbles are cheaper here than the IF PC-tag compares, stored-target mux, and EX mispredict-repair plumbing needed to remove them.\n- 2026-10-04 hyp-20261004-001-r4s0 (regression, -0.94%): Eliding load-to-store-data bubbles did not pay on CoreMark because opcode-aware load-use masking and MEM/WB store-data forwarding add hazard/MEM muxing for a dependency pattern too sparse to recover the timing cost.\n- 2026-10-04 hyp-20261004-002-r4s1 (improvement, +7.50%): Splitting LOAD/STORE rs1+imm onto a latched EX/MEM effective_addr rail improves timing by letting dmem address, mask, alignment, load extraction, and RVFI mem_addr bypass the full ALU/result mux cone, but loads must be blocked from EX/MEM forwarding because their data is not available there.\n- 2026-10-04 hyp-20261004-003-r4s2 (regression, -24.17%): Shrinking a learned loop predictor to one entry still regresses if `loop_pred_target` is carried through IF/ID and ID/EX for a 32-bit EX next-PC compare; exact-PC loop predictors need target muxing kept local to IF and only a hit bit in the repair path.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
