{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,220p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261004-002-r6s1.yaml\"","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261004-002-r6s1\ntitle: \"Pipeline the execute multiplier\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The current champion's worst path is the forwarded execute operand path\n  through u_alu into the registered multiply result rail. Prior structural\n  wins came from moving selection across pipeline boundaries while preserving\n  the one-instruction-per-cycle schedule for common ALU, load/store, branch,\n  and jump instructions. The next structural timing target is therefore the\n  remaining 32x32 multiply cone, not another front-end predictor or a broad\n  pipeline rewrite.\nhypothesis: |\n  Move all MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU work out of alu.sv's combinational\n  EX-to-EX/MEM rail and into a small registered multiply sidecar. On a multiply\n  instruction, EX captures the already-forwarded operands, op, PC/RVFI payload,\n  and writeback metadata, injects a short execute bubble, then advances the\n  instruction with the registered multiply result on the existing WB_SEL_MUL\n  rail. Simple ALU ops, branch/jump resolution, the dedicated load/store AGU,\n  the divider path, and MEM/WB writeback selection stay structurally unchanged.\n\n  This intentionally pays about one extra cycle per multiply instead of the\n  large multi-cycle penalty that previously hurt CoreMark when all M-extension\n  operations were moved into a sequential unit. In exchange, Gowin no longer\n  has to close the regfile/forwarding-to-DSP-to-EX/MEM path that currently\n  limits Fmax. CoreMark's multiply density appears low enough that a moderate\n  cycle-count increase should be outweighed by the expected clock gain.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 8\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/alu.sv\n    description: \"Remove the core's dependency on combinational multiply outputs from the hot ALU rail while preserving standalone ALU behavior or formal ALTOPS compatibility as needed.\"\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \"Add the multiply sidecar request/response control, capture forwarded operands and RVFI payload across the short MUL bubble, and feed the completed result into the existing WB_SEL_MUL rail.\"\n  - file: rtl/mul_pipe.sv\n    description: \"Add a DSP-friendly registered RV32M multiply unit for MUL, MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU, including RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS substitute results.\"\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: \"Reuse the execute-busy stall path for the one-cycle multiply bubble without adding new front-end control loops.\"\n  - file: test/test_mul_pipe.py\n    description: \"Cover low-half and high-half multiply results, signedness edge cases, dependent forwarding after the bubble, and reset/flush behavior.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
