{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_6", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"jq -r 'select(.category==\\\"structural\\\") | \\\"ID: \\\\(.id)\\\\nTITLE: \\\\(.title)\\\\nHYP: \\\\((.hypothesis.hypothesis // .hypothesis) | gsub(\\\"\\\\n\\\";\\\" \\\"))\\\\nLESSON: \\\\(.lesson // \\\"\\\")\\\\n\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"", "aggregated_output": "ID: hyp-20261004-002-r1s1\nTITLE: Split M-extension out of the EX ALU\nHYP: Restructure execute into two function-unit paths: a single-cycle RV32I ALU/branch/address path for the common operations, and a separate multi-cycle M-extension unit that accepts MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU/DIV/DIVU/ REM/REMU and stalls the front of the pipeline until its result is ready. This removes the divider and wide multiplier cones from the normal EX critical path while preserving exact RV32M semantics and RVFI ordering. CoreMark may take extra cycles on multiply-heavy regions, but the baseline Fmax is so dominated by the arithmetic cone that a large Fmax recovery should more than repay the occasional M-unit stalls. \nLESSON: - 2026-10-04 hyp-20261004-002-r1s1 (regression, +415.68%): Splitting all RV32M ops into a front-end-stalling multi-cycle unit regresses CoreMark because its MUL-heavy hot loops lose single-cycle throughput; keep MUL on the fast path and move only rare DIV/REM off the EX ALU cone.\n\nID: hyp-20261004-002-r2s1\nTITLE: Register the IF/ID boundary\nHYP: Turn IF/ID into a real registered pipeline boundary with explicit valid, enable, and kill behavior. Fetch should capture pc/instruction only when the instruction bus is ready and the downstream stage can accept it, hold the registered payload during dmem/divider/load-use stalls, and clear only the valid bit on redirect or front-end bubble. The hazard unit should compare load-use hazards against the registered IF/ID instruction and its valid bit instead of a redirect-masked combinational instruction coming out of IF. Redirect can still update the PC immediately and flush the two younger instructions, but the EX-to-IF-to-hazard-to-ID same-cycle feedback path is removed. The expected win is higher Fmax from a cleaner register-to-register control boundary while preserving single-cycle MUL throughput and the current single-retire RVFI contract. \nLESSON: - 2026-10-04 hyp-20261004-002-r2s1 (regression, -4.16%): Registering IF/ID deepened the front end and made redirects/front-end bubb\n…[3839 of 6911 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:db1122df2cb845ac]…\nayload, and writeback metadata, injects a short execute bubble, then advances the instruction with the registered multiply result on the existing WB_SEL_MUL rail. Simple ALU ops, branch/jump resolution, the dedicated load/store AGU, the divider path, and MEM/WB writeback selection stay structurally unchanged.  This intentionally pays about one extra cycle per multiply instead of the large multi-cycle penalty that previously hurt CoreMark when all M-extension operations were moved into a sequential unit. In exchange, Gowin no longer has to close the regfile/forwarding-to-DSP-to-EX/MEM path that currently limits Fmax. CoreMark's multiply density appears low enough that a moderate cycle-count increase should be outweighed by the expected clock gain. \nLESSON: - 2026-10-04 hyp-20261004-002-r6s1 (regression, -6.65%): Even a one-bubble registered `mul_pipe` sidecar regresses because CoreMark executes enough MULs that `ex_busy` stalls outweigh pulling the 32x32 cone off the forwarded EX-to-EX/MEM `mul_result` path.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
