{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"jq -r 'select(.id==\\\"hyp-20261004-002-r2s1\\\" or .id==\\\"hyp-20261004-002-r3s1\\\" or .id==\\\"hyp-20261004-002-r8s1\\\" or .id==\\\"hyp-20261004-002-r5s1\\\") | .id + \\\"\\\\n\\\" + .hypothesis.hypothesis + \\\"\\\\nnotes: \\\" + (.implementation_notes // \\\"\\\") + \\\"\\\\ncritical: \\\" + (.critical_path.modules|join(\\\" > \\\")) + \\\"\\\\n\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_6", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"jq -r 'select(.id==\\\"hyp-20261004-002-r2s1\\\" or .id==\\\"hyp-20261004-002-r3s1\\\" or .id==\\\"hyp-20261004-002-r8s1\\\" or .id==\\\"hyp-20261004-002-r5s1\\\") | .id + \\\"\\\\n\\\" + .hypothesis.hypothesis + \\\"\\\\nnotes: \\\" + (.implementation_notes // \\\"\\\") + \\\"\\\\ncritical: \\\" + (.critical_path.modules|join(\\\" > \\\")) + \\\"\\\\n\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261004-002-r2s1\nTurn IF/ID into a real registered pipeline boundary with explicit valid,\nenable, and kill behavior. Fetch should capture pc/instruction only when the\ninstruction bus is ready and the downstream stage can accept it, hold the\nregistered payload during dmem/divider/load-use stalls, and clear only the\nvalid bit on redirect or front-end bubble. The hazard unit should compare\nload-use hazards against the registered IF/ID instruction and its valid bit\ninstead of a redirect-masked combinational instruction coming out of IF.\nRedirect can still update the PC immediately and flush the two younger\ninstructions, but the EX-to-IF-to-hazard-to-ID same-cycle feedback path is\nremoved. The expected win is higher Fmax from a cleaner register-to-register\ncontrol boundary while preserving single-cycle MUL throughput and the current\nsingle-retire RVFI contract.\n\nnotes: \ncritical: clock_ibuf > cpu/u_id > cpu/u_ex > cpu/u_ex/u_alu > cpu/u_ex\n\nhyp-20261004-002-r3s1\nReplace the purely combinational IF/ID feed with a two- or four-entry\nsequential instruction skid FIFO. The fetch side keeps issuing the next\nsequential PC whenever the FIFO is not full, including during dmem/divider\nstalls that currently freeze fetch, and pushes returned instructions only\nwhen io_imemReady is high. The decode side consumes one buffered instruction\nwhenever ID can accept it; if the FIFO is empty it presents an invalid\nbubble just like today's imem stall behavior. Redirects flush the FIFO and\nrestart fetching at the resolved target, so this is not a branch predictor\nand it preserves the existing RVFI order and trap behavior. The expected\nwin is higher CoreMark iterations/cycle by hiding isolated iStall cycles\nbehind already-buffered sequential instructions, with modest timing risk\nbecause the FIFO control stays in the front end and avoids the EX ALU cone.\n\nnotes: \ncritical: clock_ibuf > cpu/u_id > cpu/u_ex > cpu/u_if > cpu/u_hazard > cpu/u_id > cpu/u_if\n\nhyp-20261004-002-r5s1\nSplit the execute writeback result path into registered op-class rails and move the final result selection after the EX/MEM boundary. Keep a compact simple-ALU rail for RV32I arithmetic and logic, keep single-cycle multiply on its own rail, keep jump return-address and divider-complete values on separate rails, and carry a small writeback-select code through MEM/WB so wb_stage and EX/MEM forwarding choose among already-registered results. This removes the large ALU case tree / MUL / DIV / JAL mux from the setup path into reg_q.alu_result while preserving single-cycle MUL throughput, current load/store AGU behavior, RVFI order, and CoreMark IPC.\n\nnotes: \ncritical: clock_ibuf > cpu/u_id > cpu/u_ex > cpu/u_ex/u_alu > cpu/u_ex\n\nhyp-20261004-002-r8s1\nRemove the core datapath's combinational multiply product from the EX-stage\nsetup path.  Let EX classify MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU and carry the already\nforwarded rs1/rs2 values plus alu_op in EX/MEM, then compute the exact\nmultiply variant in MEM and latch it into the existing MEM/WB mul_result rail.\nIndependent multiply instructions can still issue every cycle because MEM can\ncompute one product per cycle from registered EX/MEM operands, while only a\nconsumer immediately following a multiply needs a load-like one-cycle bubble\nso it can receive the result from MEM/WB.  This should cut the current\nregfile/forwarding-to-DSP-to-EX/MEM timing path without paying the full IPC\npenalty of the failed pipelined multiplier sidecar.\n\nnotes: \ncritical: clock_ibuf > cpu/u_id > cpu/u_ex > cpu/u_if > cpu/u_id\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
