{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,220p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261004-002-r9s1.yaml\"","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261004-002-r9s1\ntitle: \"Posted store buffer for dmem stalls\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The accepted core now keeps single-cycle MUL throughput and has already split\n  the main execute result rails, so the remaining accepted structural wins are\n  harder to find on the pure ALU path.  The external benchmark injects roughly\n  22 percent random dmem backpressure, and the current hazard logic freezes the\n  entire pipeline for both loads and stores whenever a memory op in MEM sees\n  io_dmemReady low.  Stores have no register result for younger integer work to\n  consume, so stalling the whole pipe until the write bus accepts the store is\n  conservative but leaves CoreMark cycles on the table.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add a one-entry posted store buffer at the MEM boundary.  When a legal store\n  reaches MEM while dmem is not ready, capture its address, replicated data,\n  mask, and RVFI metadata, let the store retire once into MEM/WB, and allow\n  younger non-memory instructions to continue while the buffer drains to the\n  dmem port on the first ready cycle.  Preserve simple in-order semantics by\n  stalling any younger load, younger store when the buffer is full, or MMIO\n  marker/UART store until the buffer has drained and the visible write can be\n  accepted.  This should hide a meaningful fraction of CoreMark's random store\n  stalls without touching the single-cycle multiply path or adding predictor\n  feedback into fetch.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: \"Add compact metadata if needed to distinguish posted stores from loads/MMIO stores and to carry a buffered-store drain state cleanly.\"\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: \"Implement the one-entry posted store buffer, arbitrate dmem outputs between the draining store and the current MEM request, and ensure a buffered store retires only once.\"\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: \"Replace the single dmem_stall condition with separate load, store-buffer-full, store-buffer-draining, and ordered-MMIO stall reasons so ordinary posted stores do not freeze fetch/decode.\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Wire store-buffer status between MEM and the hazard network while preserving the existing nret=1 top-level IO contract.\"\n  - file: test/test_store_buffer.py\n    description: \"Cover stalled stores followed by independent ALU work, loads waiting for buffered stores, full-buffer backpressure, MMIO marker stores draining in order, and RVFI single-retirement behavior.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
