mux from pc+4 to the stored target; there is no\ninstruction decode, B-immediate reconstruction, counter table, or target\nadder in fetch. Carry only a predicted-taken bit into ID/EX. EX keeps the\nexisting architectural branch resolution and redirects on the two mismatch\ncases: predicted taken but actually not taken redirects to pc+4, while not\npredicted but actually taken redirects to the resolved target. This should\nrecover most repeated loop-backedge bubbles seen by the failed static\npredictor while avoiding the long IF/EX plumbing that caused its Fmax loss.\n\nchanges:\n - rtl/core_pkg.sv Add a single predicted_taken metadata bit to IF/ID and ID/EX payloads for branch recovery.\n - rtl/if_stage.sv Add the tiny exact-tag loop-target predictor lookup and choose the stored target on a hit without decoding the fetched instruction.\n - rtl/ex_stage.sv Train the predictor from resolved taken backward branches and redirect only when predicted_taken disagrees with the resolved branch outcome.\n - rtl/core.sv Wire predictor lookup metadata and EX-stage training signals while preserving the nret=1 top-level contract.\n - test/test_loop_predictor.py Cover repeated backward \n…[4412 of 7484 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:aaebc2840bbe4a60]…\n cycles in CoreMark's load/store-heavy code with only a small mux/comparator on the store-data side.\n\nchanges:\n - rtl/hazard_unit.sv Make load-use detection source-aware: stall for rs1 consumers and for rs2 consumers that need the value in EX, but allow load-to-store-data pairs to proceed.\n - rtl/mem_stage.sv Add a MEM-stage store-data forwarding mux from the retained MEM/WB writeback value when a store's rs2 matches the just-loaded destination, including held dmem-stall cycles.\n - rtl/core.sv Wire the MEM/WB forwarding enable, destination, and existing writeback mux result into mem_stage without changing the nret=1 top-level contract.\n - test/test_load_store_forward.py Cover load-to-store-data forwarding, load-to-store-address stalls, OP-IMM false-rs2 hazards, and behavior under dmem stalls.\ncritical: {'from': 'cpu/u_rf/regs_regs_0_0_s0', 'to': 'dmem_1_dmem_1_0_0_s', 'slack_ns': -14.684, 'arrival_ns': 24.979, 'logic_cells': 13, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_id', 'cpu/u_ex', 'cpu/u_ex/u_alu', 'cpu/u_ex']}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
