{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_10", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"jq -r 'select(.category==\\\"predictor\\\") | \\\"### \\\\(.id) \\\\(.title)\\\\n\\\" + (.hypothesis.hypothesis // .hypothesis) + \\\"\\\\nLESSON: \\\" + (.lesson // \\\"\\\") + \\\"\\\\n\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"", "aggregated_output": "### hyp-20261004-003-r1s2 Tiny BTB next-PC predictor\nAdd a small direct-mapped next-PC predictor in IF: a 16-entry BTB indexed by\nfetch PC, with a modest tag, predicted target, and 2-bit taken counter. IF\nuses a valid tag hit with a taken counter to fetch the predicted target;\notherwise it fetches PC+4. EX computes the architectural next PC exactly as\ntoday, compares it with the predicted next PC carried down the pipe, and only\nredirects/flushes on a mismatch. Update the BTB in EX for conditional\nbranches, JAL, and JALR; train conditional counters up/down and force jumps\nstrongly taken. This should remove most recurring loop and call/return\nredirects while adding only a small PC-indexed lookup outside the current\nforwarding-to-ALU worst path.\n\nLESSON: - 2026-10-04 hyp-20261004-003-r1s2 (regression, -1.24%): A 16-entry direct-mapped BTB/counter next-PC predictor did not repay its IF-stage lookup and predicted-PC plumbing on CoreMark, so front-end predictor cost must be measured before assuming hot loops will offset EX redirect penalties.\n\n### hyp-20261004-003-r2s2 Table-free backward branch predictor\nAdd a table-free static backward-taken, forward-not-taken predictor for\nconditional branches in IF. Decode only the BRANCH opcode and the sign of\nits B-immediate; if the branch offset is negative, set the fetch PC to\npc+imm, otherwise keep pc+4. Carry the predicted next PC through ID/EX, and\nhave EX redirect only when the resolved architectural next PC differs from\nthat prediction. CoreMark's inner loops should avoid most taken-backedge\nredirect bubbles, while the implementation avoids the BTB tags, counters,\nand update path that hurt the prior predictor attempt.\n\nLESSON: - 2026-10-04 hyp-20261004-003-r2s2 (regression, -12.09%): Table-free backward-taken prediction still regressed CoreMark because IF-stage B-immediate decode/pc+imm selection plus pred_next_pc compare plumbing lengthened the fetch/control path more than saved back-edge redirect bubbles.\n\n### hyp-20261004-003-r3s2 Tiny learned loop-target predictor\nAdd \n…[13385 of 16457 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:5767b1c1dd9b2aab]…\nrray by a small sequential index, ignores\nio_imemReady for those hits, and predicts the branch at loop_end taken by\nwrapping the fetch PC back to loop_start.\n\nEX still resolves the loop branch exactly. If the branch exits, or if any\nredirect/jump leaves the learned range, replay mode is cancelled and the\nexisting redirect path repairs to the architectural PC. The miss path and all\nnon-loop control flow remain the current static not-taken design. This keeps\nthe common fetch path to one loop-active mux plus an entry equality check,\navoids BTB counters and predicted-target tokens, and targets both taken\nbackedge bubbles and the random imem stalls inside CoreMark hot loops.\n\nLESSON: - 2026-10-04 hyp-20261004-003-r13s2 (regression, -8.77%): Hot-loop replay regressed because IF still paid per-fetch loop range/index/fill-mask control and an instruction replay mux, while `imem_ready_eff` stall plumbing and `loop_pred_wrap` EX repair sidebands taxed every CoreMark fetch more than skipped loop-body imem stalls saved.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
