 \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove single-cycle DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU operators from the combinational ALU and leave only RV32I plus single-cycle multiply results on the fast path.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Instantiate/control an iterative divider for DIV/REM ops, hold the EX/MEM register\n…[27511 of 30583 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:9b2b07b7b88686d4]…\nnext_pc compare plumbing lengthened the fetch/control path more than saved back-edge redirect bubbles.\"}\ncores/bench/LESSONS.md:3:- 2026-10-04 hyp-20261004-003-r1s2 (regression, -1.24%): A 16-entry direct-mapped BTB/counter next-PC predictor did not repay its IF-stage lookup and predicted-PC plumbing on CoreMark, so front-end predictor cost must be measured before assuming hot loops will offset EX redirect penalties.\ncores/bench/LESSONS.md:5:- 2026-10-04 hyp-20261004-002-r2s1 (regression, -4.16%): Registering IF/ID deepened the front end and made redirects/front-end bubbles carry an extra valid/kill stage, so CoreMark's branch/control cost outweighed any timing relief from cutting the IF-to-hazard loop.\ncores/bench/LESSONS.md:6:- 2026-10-04 hyp-20261004-003-r2s2 (regression, -12.09%): Table-free backward-taken prediction still regressed CoreMark because IF-stage B-immediate decode/pc+imm selection plus pred_next_pc compare plumbing lengthened the fetch/control path more than saved back-edge redirect bubbles.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
